@startuml 07-hue-algorithm
' Source of truth for docs/images/07-hue-algorithm.svg — regenerated by
' .github/workflows/render-diagrams.yml. Edit this file, never the SVG.
skinparam backgroundColor white
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam roundCorner 8
skinparam DefaultFontName "Segoe UI"
skinparam ArrowColor #33475B
skinparam ArrowFontColor #33475B
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #FBEFD5
skinparam NoteBorderColor #A9822C
skinparam LegendBackgroundColor #F4F7FB
skinparam LegendBorderColor #33475B
skinparam activity {
  BackgroundColor #F4F7FB
  BorderColor #33475B
  DiamondBackgroundColor #EFEAE0
  DiamondBorderColor #6B5B3E
}

title hueForOrdinal(ordinal) = vanDerCorput(ordinal) × 360°\n(bit-reversal binary fraction — cover-hue.ts)

start
:bits = ordinal >>> 0;
:result = 0;
:denominator = 1;

while (bits > 0?) is (yes)
  :denominator *= 2;
  :result += (bits & 1) / denominator;
  :bits >>>= 1;
endwhile (no)

note right
  **Worked example — ordinal = 5 (0b101)**
  |= step |= bits (before shift) |= extracted bit |= denominator |= result |
  | 1 | 101 | 1 | 2 | 0.500 |
  | 2 | 010 | 0 | 4 | 0.500 |
  | 3 | 001 | 1 | 8 | 0.625 |
  Loop stops once bits == 0 — result = 5/8.
end note

:hue = result × 360°;
stop

legend bottom
  |= ordinal |= binary |= reversed, as a fraction |= hue |
  | 0 | 0 | 0/2 = 0.000 | 0° |
  | 1 | 1 | 1/2 = 0.500 | 180° |
  | 2 | 10 | 1/4 = 0.250 | 90° |
  | 3 | 11 | 3/4 = 0.750 | 270° |
  | 4 | 100 | 1/8 = 0.125 | 45° |
  | 5 | 101 | 5/8 = 0.625 | 225° |
  | 6 | 110 | 3/8 = 0.375 | 135° |
  | 7 | 111 | 7/8 = 0.875 | 315° |
  Each extra bit only ever halves the previous step size, so the Nth category
  always bisects the LARGEST remaining gap on the circle — the minimum gap
  between any n assigned hues never drops below half of the ideal 360/n
  ceiling, and hits that ceiling exactly whenever n is a power of two
  (proven for n up to 64 in cover-hue.test.ts).
  ==
  **Why not the golden angle (137.508°)?** It optimizes the FULL infinite
  sequence, not a small finite one — its worst-case minimum gap for the
  handful of categories a personal blog actually has computes to only ~38%
  of the 360/n ceiling, worse than van der Corput's guaranteed 50%.
end legend

@enduml
